The reasons of the intentional deviations:
  c3-reveal-lock: The core refuses the reveal until the answers of the round are locked in; the reference implementation lets the store change the phase without the lock. The refusal is the anti-spoiler guarantee of the base.
    source: docs/design/PLATFORM-ARCHITECTURE.md § 10.2 (rule C-3); REQUIREMENTS.md (GAME-002); core/phomu-core/src/session/answers.rs (reveal)
    fields: phases, currentRound, currentSongId, roundCount, error
  a6-locked-in-phase: The core enters the phase `locked-in` when the last expected answer arrives; the reference implementation types the phase but never enters it. The core closes the deviation A6 of the contract — the reveal is only released when the answers are locked in.
    source: docs/design/GAME-CONTRACT.md § 2.1 and § 11.1 (deviation A6); REQUIREMENTS.md (GAME-002); core/phomu-core/src/session/answers.rs (lock_answers)
    fields: phases
  answer-without-player: The core refuses an answer when the turn order is empty — there is nobody to attribute the answer to. The reference implementation evaluates the mode and applies its effect without recording the answer.
    source: core/phomu-core/src/session/answers.rs (submit_answer); docs/spec/REQUIREMENTS-PLATFORM.md (CORE-111)
    fields: timelineYears, error

------------------------------------------------------------------------
DOCUMENTED DIVERGENCES — the corpus marks them as not portable
------------------------------------------------------------------------
[phase/locked-in-declared] (area: phase-machine)
  reason: The phase `locked-in` is declared but entered by no flow (GAME-CONTRACT section 2.1, deviation A6). The TypeScript store accepts it; the Rust core decides whether an undeclared transition is a typed error (CORE-111). The divergence is intentional and must be decided by the owner (OPEN-08).
  rust-vs-typescript    phases[0]: typescript "locked-in", rust "question"
  rust-vs-typescript    error: typescript null, rust {"code":"1102","message":"errors.answersNotLocked"}
[error/answer-without-current-song] (area: errors)
  reason: The error vocabulary of the vector layer belongs to the runner, not to the game: the two runners name the same failure differently. The situation is also one that only the reference can reach — the core cannot enter the question phase without a drawn song. The vector therefore documents the behaviour of the reference and is not compared.
[error/award-points-unknown-player] (area: errors)
  reason: The TypeScript reference ignores the operation silently (game-store.ts:463 `if (!player) return state`). The Rust core is required to return a typed error (CORE-111). The divergence is intentional: the core is stricter than the reference.
  rust-vs-typescript    error: typescript null, rust {"code":"1004","message":"errors.unknownPlayer"}
[error/remove-unknown-timeline-year] (area: errors)
  reason: The TypeScript reference ignores the operation silently (game-store.ts:611 `if (idx === -1) return {}`). The Rust core is required to return a typed error (CORE-111). The divergence is intentional: the core is stricter than the reference.
  rust-vs-typescript    error: typescript null, rust {"code":"1007","message":"errors.invalidAnswer"}
[divergence/mode-rotation-random] (area: divergence)
  reason: The mode rotation draws from `Math.random()` (game-store.ts:190). The Rust core uses a seeded generator (CORE-113), but a seeded generator with a different algorithm produces a different sequence. The vector therefore uses a single mode in every portable case; the rotation itself is not comparable until the owner defines a shared algorithm and seed.
[divergence/shifting-teams-random] (area: divergence)
  reason: The shifting team mode re-shuffles the players with `Math.random()` (game-store.ts:353). The membership of the teams after the switch is therefore not predictable — and the reference keeps the points of the teams and the players (deviation A8). The vector records the score behaviour, which is deterministic; the membership is not part of the observation.
  rust-vs-typescript    teamScores.t1: typescript 5, rust 8
  rust-vs-typescript    teamScores.t2: typescript 3, rust 0
  rust-vs-typescript    playerTeams.p2: typescript "t2", rust "t1"
  rust-vs-typescript    playerTeams.p3: typescript "t1", rust "t2"
  rust-vs-typescript    teamMembers.t1[0]: typescript "p3", rust "p2"
  rust-vs-typescript    teamMembers.t2[0]: typescript "p2", rust "p3"

